        ":question/id": "2e87292a-2b40-5740-b2bc-7f066927b72e",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,-2),(2,-2)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(-1)}=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,2)}=-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e6b2df4680cea04cda42312659c76ba713a025150ebcc547dddc2a6501aebf3.png)",
